# Amazon EKS Zero to Hero (2026 Edition)

# Chapter 3 – Linux Fundamentals for EKS

## Learning Objectives

After completing this chapter, you will understand:

* Linux operating system basics
* Linux directory structure
* File and directory management
* Users and permissions
* Processes and services
* Package management
* Networking commands
* SSH
* Environment variables
* Shell scripting basics
* Linux troubleshooting commands

Linux knowledge is essential because:

* Kubernetes nodes run Linux.
* Container images are mostly Linux-based.
* Most production troubleshooting happens through Linux commands.

---

# 1. What is Linux?

Linux is an open-source operating system used widely in:

* Cloud servers
* Data centers
* Kubernetes nodes
* Containers
* Embedded systems

Examples of Linux distributions:

* Ubuntu
* Amazon Linux
* Red Hat Enterprise Linux
* Debian
* CentOS

AWS EC2 commonly uses:

* Amazon Linux
* Ubuntu

---

# 2. Linux Architecture

Basic Linux architecture:

```text
+----------------------+
| Applications         |
+----------------------+
| Shell                |
+----------------------+
| Kernel               |
+----------------------+
| Hardware             |
+----------------------+
```

---

## Kernel

The kernel is the core of Linux.

It manages:

* CPU
* Memory
* Storage
* Network
* Hardware communication

Example:

```text
Application

↓

Kernel

↓

Hardware
```

---

## Shell

Shell provides a way to communicate with Linux.

Examples:

* bash
* zsh
* sh

Example:

```bash
ls
```

Shell sends instructions to the kernel.

---

# 3. Connecting to Linux Server

Most cloud servers are accessed using SSH.

Example:

```bash
ssh username@server-ip
```

Example:

```bash
ssh ec2-user@10.0.1.25
```

---

# 4. Linux File System

Linux uses a tree structure.

```text
/

├── bin
├── boot
├── dev
├── etc
├── home
├── opt
├── tmp
├── usr
└── var
```

---

# Important Directories

## /

Root directory.

Everything starts here.

---

## /home

User files.

Example:

```text
/home/john
```

---

## /etc

Configuration files.

Examples:

```text
/etc/passwd

/etc/hosts

/etc/ssh
```

---

## /var

Variable data.

Contains:

* Logs
* Application data

Example:

```text
/var/log
```

---

## /tmp

Temporary files.

---

## /bin

Essential Linux commands.

Examples:

```text
ls
cp
mv
rm
```

---

