# 6. File Permissions

Linux permissions control access.

Example:

```text
-rwxr-xr--
```

Meaning:

```text
Owner   Group   Others

rwx     r-x     r--
```

---

## Permission Types

| Symbol | Meaning |
| ------ | ------- |
| r      | Read    |
| w      | Write   |
| x      | Execute |

---

# 7. chmod Command

Change permissions.

Example:

```bash
chmod 755 script.sh
```

Meaning:

```text
Owner:
rwx

Group:
r-x

Others:
r-x
```

---

Common permissions:

```text
644
```

Files:

```text
rw-r--r--
```

---

```text
755
```

Executables:

```text
rwxr-xr-x
```

---

# 8. Users and Groups

Linux is multi-user.

View users:

```bash
cat /etc/passwd
```

---

Create user:

```bash
sudo useradd developer
```

---

Switch user:

```bash
su developer
```

---

Current user:

```bash
whoami
```

---

Groups:

```bash
groups
```

---

# 9. Root User

Root has full permissions.

Example:

```text
root

↓

Can modify everything
```

Use carefully.

---

Run command as root:

```bash
sudo command
```

Example:

```bash
sudo yum update
```

---

# 10. Package Management

Linux installs software using package managers.

---

## Amazon Linux / RHEL

Using yum:

```bash
sudo yum install nginx
```

---

## Ubuntu

Using apt:

```bash
sudo apt update

sudo apt install nginx
```

---

# 11. Processes

A process is a running program.

View processes:

```bash
ps
```

Detailed:

```bash
ps aux
```

---

Real-time monitoring:

```bash
top
```

or

```bash
htop
```

---

Find process:

```bash
ps aux | grep nginx
```

---

Kill process:

```bash
kill PID
```

Force kill:

```bash
kill -9 PID
```

---

# 12. Services

Linux services run in the background.

Example:

* nginx
* docker
* kubelet

---

Check service:

```bash
systemctl status nginx
```

---

Start service:

```bash
systemctl start nginx
```

---

Stop service:

```bash
systemctl stop nginx
```

---

Restart:

```bash
systemctl restart nginx
```

---

Enable on boot:

```bash
systemctl enable nginx
```

---

# 13. Logs

Logs are critical for troubleshooting.

Common location:

```bash
/var/log
```

View logs:

```bash
cat /var/log/messages
```

Follow logs:

```bash
tail -f logfile
```

Example:

```bash
tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log
```

